## Founding & History

Freycinet Vineyard was established in 1978 by Geoff and Susan Bull, making it the first commercial winery on Tasmania's East Coast. The Bulls, originally from Sydney, moved to Bicheno, Tasmania, in 1975, where Geoff initially pursued a career as a commercial abalone diver. In 1978, they acquired the Cosgrove property near the Freycinet Peninsula, inspired by Tasmania's emerging wine industry. They planted their first vines in 1979 and produced their initial wines in 1983, with assistance from wine consultant Gary Baldwin. The vineyard's unique terroir quickly gained recognition for producing quality wines. In 2013, Freycinet Vineyard expanded its holdings by acquiring the adjoining Coombend vineyard.

## People

Freycinet Vineyard remains a family-owned and operated business. Founders Geoff and Susan Bull were instrumental in establishing the vineyard. Their daughter, Lindy, and her partner, Claudio Radenti, joined the family business in 1992. Both Lindy and Claudio are formally trained winemakers, having studied at Roseworthy College and gained experience in various wine regions globally. The next generation of the family, Giorgio, Pino, and Pia, are also involved in the vineyard's operations.

## Vineyard & Site

Freycinet Vineyard is situated adjacent to the Freycinet Peninsula, at approximately 42 degrees latitude. The vineyard encompasses 15 hectares, though some sources indicate 16 hectares. It is described as a warm, sheltered site within Tasmania's generally cool climate. The vineyards are meticulously hand-tended. The farming approach involves hand pruning, shoot thinning and positioning, leaf plucking, and hand picking to ensure moderate yields of fully ripened, healthy fruit from well-balanced vines. Varietals planted include Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Riesling, and grapes for traditional method sparkling wines. Following the acquisition of the Coombend vineyard in 2013, Shiraz and Sauvignon Blanc were added to their range. Cabernet Merlot is also produced.

